===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Characterized by having [[two]] (usually equivalent) components.
#: {{syn|en|double|twin|Thesaurus:dual}}
#: {{coi|en|a '''dual'''-motor vehicle}}
# Pertaining to [[two]], pertaining to a [[pair]] of.
#: {{syn|en|double|duplicate|Thesaurus:twofold}}
#: {{coi|en|'''dual''' engine failure}}
#: {{coi|en|'''dual''' citizenship}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020|editor=Aída Besançon Spencer; William David Spencer|title=Christian Egalitarian Leadership: Empowering the Whole Church According to the Scriptures|author=Grace Ying May|chapter=Women Disciplining Men: A Biblical Pattern of Leadership|page=48|text=Both Deborah and Samuel held '''dual''' roles as judges and prophets.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar}} Pertaining to a [[grammatical]] {{l|en|number|id=grammar}} in certain languages that refers to two of something, such as a pair of shoes.
#: {{cot|en|singular|trial|plural}}
#: {{ux|en|Modern Arabic displays a '''dual''' number, as did Homeric Greek.}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics|physics}} Exhibiting [[duality]].
# {{lb|en|linear algebra}} Being the space of all [[linear functional]]s of (some other space).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Doug Fisher; Hans-J. Lenz|title=Learning from Data: Artificial Intelligence and Statistics V|publisher=Springer Science & Business Media|isbn=9781461224044|page=81
|passage=Accordingly, a hyperplane in the sample space is '''dual''' to a subspace in the variable space.}}
# {{lb|en|category theory}} Being the [[dual#Noun|dual]] of some other category; containing the same objects but with source and target reversed for all [[morphisms]].
#: {{syn|en|opposite}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Colin McLarty|title=Elementary Categories, Elementary Toposes|publisher=Clarendon Press|isbn=9780191589492|page=77
|passage=Every category is '''dual''' to its own dual, so if a statement holds in all categories so does its dual.}}

